# Framework Context Budget

## Hard limit (product)

Cursor injects `alwaysApply: true` rules into **every** Agent turn. It cannot
gate rules by model ID. LiteLLM trim cannot remove rules Cursor already sent.

## Token taxes observed (this repo)

| Tax | Approx | Source |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Cursor builtin tool schemas | ~26k | Task/Shell/etc. (`logs/litellm-tools-capture/`) |
| Former always-on framework rules | ~45k | `.cursor/rules/*` with `alwaysApply: true` |
| Local Ornith context | 32k | vLLM behind `litellm.hom.lab` |

Always-on framework + tools alone exceeds the local window.

## Policy

### Always-on (keep tiny)

Only thin boot surfaces stay `alwaysApply: true`:

- this file
- `framework-user-interaction-style.mdc`
- `framework-ai-agent-model-lanes.mdc`
- `.cursorrules` (boot index)

### On-demand / glob (full framework)

Heavy `framework-*`, Ansible/NetBox gates, MCP briefing, troubleshooting, and
coding standards are `alwaysApply: false` (often with `globs`). Cursor provider
models with large context should load them when the task matches the rule
`description` or when matching files are open.

### Local LiteLLM / Ornith sessions

Do **not** auto-load the full framework family. Prefer:

1. User request + opened files
2. Skills under `.cursor/skills/` / `skills/` when explicitly relevant
3. One targeted rule via `@` mention if needed

If the local model needs full partner-process / plan-governance / Ansible
standards, say so and switch to a Cursor provider model (or `@` the specific
rule). Do not silently re-inject the whole framework into a 32k window.

### Separate from tools tax

Disabling framework always-on does **not** shrink Cursor’s builtin `tools[]`
schemas (~26k). That is a client/product cost; LiteLLM trim is only a safety
net after the request is built.
